#!/usr/bin/env python3.14
"""
prebuilt-binaries: smoke-test installed binary and runtime payloads.

Runs ./loadout install @shared --dest-dir /tmp/... --no-backup to get a real
deployment layout, then probes each executable in <dest>/local/bin/ with a
cut-down PATH containing only <dest>/local/bin plus basic system directories.
This catches wrappers that accidentally rely on tools from the user's
~/.local/bin or the build host.

Each executable must **exit 0** from one of its probe invocations (PROBE_FLAGS,
else DEFAULT_PROBE). A non-zero exit is accepted only through an EXPECT_NONZERO
entry, which pins the flags, the exit code and a required output substring.
Fatal output patterns ("symbol lookup error", "undefined symbol", a broken
Tcl_Init) fail even on exit 0. A timeout still counts as OK -- an interactive
binary that blocks has demonstrably loaded and started.

The rule used to be the other way round: any exit outside {126, 127, 139}
passed. That scored a binary green off its own error message, and three gtkwave
tools shipped that way -- see the comment on EXPECT_NONZERO.

It also checks runtime sentinels for shipped editor payloads and runs Neovim
headless against the installed runtime.

Exit 0: all clean.  Exit 1: failures detected (fails the pre-commit).

Usage:
    tests/prebuilt-binaries
    tests/prebuilt-binaries --keep   # don't delete tmpdir
"""

